//==
// # Tooling: law runner types
//
// Layer 9 of the STEP B-rep to mesh design (README.md beside this file): what
// a run of the laws produces. Laws compile to Boolean functions, but nothing
// in a backend builds their witness arguments and calls them (TKT-311's
// finding); the runner does that in Plato, and the host only calls
// LawReportOf and prints. A run is one law over one witness: held, failed,
// failed where the witness was built to fail it, held where it should have
// failed, or skipped with the reason.
//
// Sections, in order:
//  - Verdicts and runs
//  - Named witnesses
//  - Tables

//==
// # Verdicts and runs

// The verdict of one law over one witness. FailedAsExpected: a negative
// witness fails a law it was built to fail (layer 7's table of negative
// witnesses). HeldUnexpectedly: it holds one of those laws, which is a
// defect in the witness or the law. Skipped: the law was not evaluated,
// and Reason says why (a parameter only a generator supplies, or a cost
// the runner does not pay per call).
type LawVerdict
    = Held
    | Failed
    | FailedAsExpected
    | HeldUnexpectedly
    | Skipped(Reason: String);

// Where in the pipeline a run looked at its witness. ModelStage: the
// model's own validation (ValidateBrep at Oracle level). MeshedStage: the
// report of meshing the witness under layer 8's witness options (model,
// mesh and cross checks). MeshStage: a mesh witness's own validation.
// SelectorStage: a Law_ called directly with Integer selectors.
type RunStage
    = ModelStage
    | MeshedStage
    | MeshStage
    | SelectorStage;

// One law over one witness. Measure is the law's worst measure where it
// exports one (a Check's Worst), the first failing index for a law
// quantified over indices, and NaN where neither exists (layer 0's
// convention for an absent measure). Where is the check's location, or
// Nowhere.
type LawRun
{
    Law: String;
    Witness: String;
    Stage: RunStage;
    Verdict: LawVerdict;
    Measure: Number;
    Where: Provenance;
}

// Every run, with the backend it ran under. Backend is supplied by the
// host entry point: the writer and compiler version that produced the code
// that ran (until TKT-315's writer fixes land, nested conditionals
// miscompile in the C# backend, so a result is only as good as the writer
// named here).
type LawReport
{
    Backend: String;
    Runs: Array<LawRun>;
}

//==
// # Named witnesses

// A model witness with the name a report prints and the laws it is built
// to fail (empty for a positive witness).
type NamedModel
{
    Name: String;
    Model: BrepModel;
    ExpectedFailures: Array<String>;
}

// A mesh witness with the name a report prints.
type NamedMesh
{
    Name: String;
    Mesh: ModelMesh;
}

//==
// # Tables

// A table the host writes as CSV for the notebook (the toolkit's CSV
// source reads it): a title, column names, and rows of cells, each row as
// long as Columns.
type ResultTable
{
    Title: String;
    Columns: Array<String>;
    Rows: Array<Array<CellValue>>;
}
